{"qid":"Q298","names":["Republic of Chile"],"title":"Republic of Chile","endonym":{"native":"Chile","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Chile declared independence from Spain in 1818 following Bernardo O'Higgins's victory at the Battle of Chacabuco (1817), and the dataset attests the republic from 1812, coinciding with the first Patria Vieja government. The War of the Pacific (1879-1884) against Bolivia and Peru was the defining territorial event of the nineteenth century, awarding Chile the nitrate-rich Atacama and Tarapaca regions and giving it a decisive Pacific strategic position. The parliamentary era (1891-1925) gave way to authoritarian episodes, including Augusto Pinochet's military government (1973-1990), after which democracy was restored. Chile remains one of the most stable economies in Latin America in the twenty-first century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern Chilean boundaries are precisely defined by international treaties, particularly post-War of the Pacific agreements; the Atacama Desert frontier with Bolivia and the Patagonian boundary with Argentina were settled definitively only in 1881 and thereafter, so pre-1881 geometry is approximate for those sectors.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Chile","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Chile"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Chile","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chile"}],"britannica":"place/Chile","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Chile","rulers":[{"qid":"Q533455","name":"José Miguel Infante","start":1813,"end":1814,"image":"Jose Miguel Infante.jpeg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jose%20Miguel%20Infa